 2. $40 trillion milestone 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: U.S. Treasury; Chart: Danielle Alberti/Axios The chart above shows one key reason yesterday's decline in borrowing costs from the intervention announcement didn't stick. 

 Federal debt crossed the largely symbolic $40 trillion mark this week, an especially fitting milestone amid the turmoil in long-term bonds. Why it matters: Treasury can intervene in the bond market. But the U.S. government's enormous deficits and borrowing needs remain.

 What they're saying: "A ballooning deficit that has little-to-no-prospects of going away has had a hand in driving yields higher," Hardika Singh, an economic strategist at Fundstrat Research, wrote in a note this morning. 

 "[M]aking yields go down over the longer period will require the painful work of bringing down the debt," Singh added. What to watch: Bessent told CNBC this morning that there is "a very good chance we have" seen peak deficits of the Trump administration.

 Bessent pointed to tariff refunds as a temporary factor widening the deficit, with tariff revenue expected to resume as tariffs come back into effect. By the numbers: The government ran a $1.8 trillion deficit last year and is on track for another roughly $2 trillion shortfall in 2026, according to the Congressional Budget Office.

 Persistent deficits mean Treasury will have to keep adding to that $40 trillion debt pile at a time when long-term borrowing costs are near multidecade highs. 
 
